{{ElectionsIA}}
The '''2026 United States Senate election in Iowa''' was held on November 3, 2026, to elect a member of the [[United States Senate]] to represent the state of [[Iowa]]. [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] state representative [[Josh Turek]] defeated [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] representative [[Ashley Hinson]]. Republican incumbent [[Joni Ernst]] did not seek a third term.<ref name="ErnstRetire" />

[[Primary elections]] were held on June 2. Endorsed by President [[Donald Trump]], Hinson won the Republican nomination with 73.9% of the vote over former state senator [[Jim Carlin (politician)|Jim Carlin]]. Turek won the Democratic nomination with 62.6% of the vote over state senator [[Zach Wahls]].

Turek is the first Democrat to win a Senate election in Iowa since [[2008 United States Senate election in Iowa|2008]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Could Iowa Democrats Win 2026's Gubernatorial and US Senate Races? |url=https://smartpolitics.lib.umn.edu/2026/06/03/could-iowa-democrats-win-2026s-gubernatorial-and-us-senate-races/ |work=Smart Politics |date=June 3, 2026 |access-date=June 6, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fox9.com/news/iowa-elections-democrats-flip-seats-see-who-won-key-races|title=Iowa elections: Democrats flip seats, see who won key races|publisher=[[KMSP-TV]]|last=Jackson|first=Ashleigh|date=November 4, 2026|access-date=November 16, 2026}}</ref>

This was one of eight seats Democrats flipped in a state carried by [[Donald Trump]] in the [[2024 United States presidential election|2024 presidential election]].

== Background ==
Since voting for President [[Barack Obama]] in both [[2008 United States presidential election in Iowa|2008]] and [[2012 United States presidential election in Iowa|2012]], Iowa had trended increasingly Republican and was considered a moderately to strongly [[red state]] at the federal and state level. Republican nominee [[Donald Trump]] won Iowa in [[2020 United States presidential election in Iowa|2020]] by 8 percentage points, and in [[2024 United States presidential election in Iowa|2024]] grew his margin to 13 percentage points. Republicans controlled every statewide executive office (except the [[Iowa Auditor of State|state auditor's office]]), majorities in both chambers of the [[Iowa State Legislature|state legislature]], and the entire congressional delegation.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ta |first=Linh |date=2024-11-06 |title=Iowa remains solidly Republican |url=https://www.axios.com/local/des-moines/2024/11/06/iowa-remains-red-despite-iowa-poll-republicans |access-date=2024-12-13 |website=[[Axios (website)|Axios]] |language=en}}</ref>  Incumbent Senator Joni Ernst was [[2014 United States Senate election in Iowa|first elected]] as part of the [[2014 United States Senate elections|2014]] [[Wave elections in the United States|red wave]]<ref>{{Cite web |last=Barrett |first=Ted |date=2014-11-05 |title=Joni Ernst wins Iowa Senate race |url=https://www.cnn.com/2014/11/05/politics/iowa-senate-digital/index.html |access-date=2024-12-13 |website=[[CNN]] |language=en}}</ref> and was reelected in [[2020 United States Senate election in Iowa|2020]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Pfannenstiel |first=Brianne |title='Six more years!': Republican U.S. Sen. Joni Ernst wins reelection, beating Democrat Theresa Greenfield |url=https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/2020/11/02/iowa-election-results-us-senate-theresa-greenfield-joni-ernst/5997461002/ |access-date=December 13, 2024 |date=November 2, 2020 |website=[[The Des Moines Register]] |language=en-US}}</ref>

In the latter half of her second term, Ernst came under increasing pressure.  In December 2024, she faced pushback from Republicans for her skepticism of [[Pete Hegseth]]'s nomination for [[United States Secretary of Defense|Secretary of Defense]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Copp |first1=Tara |title=Sen. Joni Ernst wants to hear from Hegseth on sex assault in the military and women in combat |url=https://apnews.com/article/ernst-hegseth-nominee-trump-vetting-assault-pentagon-138c79c9199c8525d43b77417674d81e |access-date=17 October 2026 |agency=Associated Press |date=December 7, 2024}}</ref>  She later sparked controversy for replying "we are all going to die" when a constituent confronted her for deaths that could be caused by cuts to [[Medicaid]] and [[Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program|SNAP]], which she supported as part of the [[One Big Beautiful Bill Act]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Wise |first=Alana |date=May 31, 2025 |title=Sen. Ernst doubles down on bleak response to Medicaid grilling |url=https://www.npr.org/2025/05/31/nx-s1-5418932/we-all-are-going-to-die-ernst-joni-town-hall-iowa-senator |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250531221645/https://www.npr.org/2025/05/31/nx-s1-5418932/we-all-are-going-to-die-ernst-joni-town-hall-iowa-senator |archive-date=May 31, 2025 |website=[[NPR]]}}</ref> 

In September 2025, Ernst announced that she would not seek reelection, officially citing family obligations, although her decision was interpreted in light of previous controversies.  The resulting open seat was expected to make the race more competitive.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Opsahl |first1=Robin |title=U.S. Sen. Joni Ernst announces she will not seek reelection |url=https://iowacapitaldispatch.com/2025/09/02/u-s-sen-joni-ernst-announces-she-will-not-seek-reelection/ |access-date=17 October 2026 |work=Iowa Capital Dispatch |agency=Iowa Capital Dispatch |date=2 September 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=The Associated Press |title=Sen. Joni Ernst of Iowa confirms she will not run for reelection in 2026 |url=https://www.mprnews.org/story/2025/09/02/sen-joni-ernst-of-iowa-confirms-she-will-not-run-for-reelection-in-2026 |website=MPR News |access-date=17 October 2026 |language=en |date=2 September 2025}}</ref>

