== General election ==
According to ''The Daily Iowan'', the [[cost of living]], [[inflation]], [[Tariff|tariffs]], [[Universal health care by country|health-care affordability]], [[agriculture]], and [[AI data center|AI data centers]] are contested issues of the campaign.<ref name="Olson12">{{Cite web |last=Olson |first=Anna |title=Hinson, Turek debate farms, Iowa data centers in last debate |url=https://dailyiowan.com/2026/10/13/hinson-turek-debate-farms-iowa-data-centers-in-last-debate/ |access-date=2026-10-28 |website=The Daily Iowan}}</ref> Debates focused on which candidate was most capable to handle the [[United States affordability crisis|American affordability crisis]].<ref name="Olson12" /><ref name="News-1">{{Cite news |last=Wagner |first=Bayliss |date=2026-10-30 |title=Democrats, Hopeful All Over the Senate Map, Remain Jittery About Iowa |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/10/30/us/politics/iowa-senate-race-josh-turek.html |access-date=2026-10-31 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> Turek has said he would reverse the [[Medicaid coverage gap|Medicaid spending cuts]] passed under the [[One Big Beautiful Bill Act]].<ref name=":32">{{Cite news |last=Gruber-Miller|first=Stephen|date=March 10, 2026|title=Josh Turek hears rural health care concerns, pitches public option|url=https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/elections/2026/03/10/us-senate-candidate-josh-turek-des-moines/88946181007/|access-date=April 22, 2026|work=[[The Des Moines Register]]}}</ref><ref name=":122">{{Cite news |last=Robin|first=Opsahl|date=August 12, 2025|title=Iowa Rep. Josh Turek, two-time Paralympian, launches campaign for U.S. Senate|url=https://iowacapitaldispatch.com/2025/08/12/iowa-rep-josh-turek-two-time-paralympian-launches-campaign-for-u-s-senate/|access-date=April 22, 2026|work=[[Iowa Capital Dispatch]]}}</ref><ref name=":3">{{Cite news |last=Gruber-Miller|first=Stephen|date=March 10, 2026|title=Josh Turek hears rural health care concerns, pitches public option|url=https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/elections/2026/03/10/us-senate-candidate-josh-turek-des-moines/88946181007/|access-date=April 22, 2026|work=[[The Des Moines Register]]}}</ref><ref name=":12">{{Cite news |last=Robin|first=Opsahl|date=August 12, 2025|title=Iowa Rep. Josh Turek, two-time Paralympian, launches campaign for U.S. Senate|url=https://iowacapitaldispatch.com/2025/08/12/iowa-rep-josh-turek-two-time-paralympian-launches-campaign-for-u-s-senate/|access-date=April 22, 2026|work=[[Iowa Capital Dispatch]]}}</ref> Hinson supported the cuts as [[Fiscal conservatism|fiscally responsible]] as well as [[Tariffs in the second Trump administration|Trump's tariffs]].<ref name="Olson12" />

Hinson ran on [[Social conservatism|socially conservative]] policies, opposing [[Anti-abortion movements|a legal right to have an abortion]] with exceptions for rape, incest, and the life of the mother and [[LGBTQ movements]].<ref name="News-1" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Gruber-Miller |first=Marissa Payne and Stephen |title=How Iowa congressional candidates are talking about abortion in 2026 |url=https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/elections/2026/01/26/abortion-iowa-2026-midterm-election/88264823007/ |access-date=2026-10-31 |website=The Des Moines Register |language=en-US}}</ref> Turek supports restoring the abortion protections that ''[[Roe v. Wade]]'' previously provided, by passing a federal law.<ref name=":32" /> He claimed that [[Abortion in Iowa|Iowa's six-week abortion ban]] reduced [[OB-GYN]] services available.<ref name=":32" /> Turek affirmed [[Conservatism|conservative]] policies on [[Right to keep and bear arms|guns]], [[transgender rights movement|transgender rights]], and [[Opposition to immigration|immigration]].<ref name="News-1" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Rooker |first=Amanda |date=2026-09-10 |title=Why Republican themes are showing up in some Iowa Democrats' ads |url=https://www.kcci.com/article/why-republican-themes-are-showing-up-in-some-iowa-democrats-ads/73659473 |access-date=2026-10-31 |website=KCCI |language=en}}</ref>

Turek criticized the concentration of [[artificial intelligence]] technology in the hands of a few companies. He supports regulating [[Data center|data centers]], opposes tax breaks for their operators, and "safeguards" for the technology.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Josh Turek says Congress has a role in regulating A.I.|url=https://www.c-span.org/clip/campaign-2026/josh-turek-says-congress-has-a-role-in-regulating-ai/5206891|format=video|work=[[C-SPAN]]|date=September 21, 2026|access-date=October 26, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Collins|first=Terry|date=30 September 2026|title=Democrats are running against data centers in key Senate races|url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/elections/2026/09/30/democrats-data-centers-regulation-ohio-iowa-texas/91498159007/?gnt-cfr=1&gca-cat=p|work=USA Today|quote=Democratic state Rep. Josh Turek, a Paralympic gold medalist basketball player running for Senate, and Iowa state auditor Rob Sand, the Democratic nominee for governor, both frame their opposition to data centers as holding large corporations accountable. "We want to make sure large corporations are paying their fair share, not getting tax breaks," Turek told reporters on Sept. 21 during a campaign stop in Des Moines. "I think also making sure we've got strong guardrails to make sure they are not driving up the energy rate to the end user."}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Lemon|first=Jason|last2=Commander|first2=Anna|date=7 October 2026|title=Turek, Hinson Clash in Iowa US Senate Debate: Key Moments, Polls|url=https://www.newsweek.com/iowa-senate-debate-ashley-hinson-josh-turek-polls-watch-12536792|work=Newsweek|quote=Turek said that AI will fundamentally transform our society. "I agree we should not cede the ground to China," he said, but added that Americans need protections for jobs and more. "I am absolutely in favor of some regulations on data centers," Turek said, while calling for no more tax breaks for companies.}}</ref> Hinson is in favor of [[Progress in artificial intelligence|AI development]], and says that [[Retraining|preparation for workers]] is necessary to adapt to [[Unemployment in the United States|AI job losses]]. She said an "AI economy" is necessary to win an [[Artificial intelligence arms race|American-Chinese AI arms race]], "because the truth is: if we don't, [[China]] will."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Feng|first=Lin|date=2026-10-20|title=From Soybeans to Data Centers, China Debate Hits US Midterm Campaigns|url=https://www.voanews.com/a/from-soybeans-to-data-centers-china-debate-hits-us-midterm-campaigns-20261020/8213314.html|access-date=2026-10-31|website=Voice of America|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Hinson|first=Ashley|date=March 20, 2026|title=Ashley Hinson Op-Ed: Congress needs to ensure that the AI future belongs to our kids, not to Beijing|url=https://www.nrsc.org/press-releases/ashley-hinson-op-ed-congress-needs-to-ensure-that-the-ai-future-belongs-to-our-kids-not-to-beijing-2026-03-20/|access-date=2026-10-31|language=en-US}}</ref>

